{"name":"Thor’s Thunder","hex":"#44ccff","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"thor","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["there","they're (they are)"]},{"part":"Pronoun","definitions":["their"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/thor"},{"term":"thunder","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The loud rumbling, cracking, or crashing sound caused by expansion of rapidly heated air around a lightning bolt.","A deep, rumbling noise resembling thunder."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To produce thunder; to sound, rattle, or roar, as a discharge of atmospheric electricity.","To make a noise like thunder."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/thunder"}],"entries":[{"title":"Thunder","blocks":[{"text":"Thunder is the sound caused by lightning. Depending upon the distance from and nature of the lightning, it can range from a long, low rumble to a sudden, loud crack. The sudden increase in temperature and hence pressure caused by the lightning produces rapid expansion of the air in the path of a lightning bolt. In turn, this expansion of air creates a sonic shock wave, often referred to as a \"thunderclap\" or \"peal of thunder\". The scientific study of thunder is known as brontology and the irrational fear (phobia) of thunder is called brontophobia."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thunder","wikidata":"Q83301"},{"title":"Thor","blocks":[{"text":"Thor is a prominent god in Germanic paganism. In Norse mythology, he is a hammer-wielding god associated with thunder, storms, strength, protection, fertility, and farmers. Besides Old Norse Þórr, the deity occurs in Old English as Thunor, in Old Frisian as Thuner, in Old Saxon as Thunar, and in Old High German as Donar, all ultimately stemming from the Proto-Germanic theonym *Þun(a)raz, meaning 'Thunder'."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/A_painting_by_Max_Friedrich_Koch_-_Donar-Thor.jpg/330px-A_painting_by_Max_Friedrich_Koch_-_Donar-Thor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thor","wikidata":"Q42952"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/AE_1971%2C282_Vorderansicht.jpg/500px-AE_1971%2C282_Vorderansicht.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/AE_1971%2C282_Vorderansicht.jpg/1280px-AE_1971%2C282_Vorderansicht.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/AE_1971%2C282_Vorderansicht.jpg/500px-AE_1971%2C282_Vorderansicht.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Altar stone for Hercules Magusanus from Bonn, dated 226 AD.[14]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAE_1971%2C282_Vorderansicht.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Bonifacius_by_Emil_Doepler.jpg/500px-Bonifacius_by_Emil_Doepler.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Bonifacius_by_Emil_Doepler.jpg/1280px-Bonifacius_by_Emil_Doepler.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Bonifacius_by_Emil_Doepler.jpg/500px-Bonifacius_by_Emil_Doepler.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Boniface bears his crucifix after felling Thor's Oak in Bonifacius (1905) by Emil Doepler","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABonifacius_by_Emil_Doepler.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Olaus_Magnus_-_On_the_three_Main_Gods_of_the_Geats.jpg/500px-Olaus_Magnus_-_On_the_three_Main_Gods_of_the_Geats.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikime